      "locked": false,
      "text": "Module 1: IP addressing, Subnetting and Supernetting\n\nLecture 1: Introduction to IP addressing\n\n-> IP Addresses are unique addresses given to computers that are connected to internet.\n-> They are given based on the link the computer is connected to.\n-> It has 32 bits. And the address itself is divided into two parts: Network ID and Host ID.\n\n\n-> Classful addressing: The IP addresses are divided into 5 classes based on the Most Significant Byte of the IP address.\n-> 0------- xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class A. Only MSB is part of Network ID.\n-> 10------ xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class B. The two MSB are part of Network ID.\n-> 110----- xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class C. The three MSB are part of Network ID.\n-> 1110---- xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class D. This is not used for computers.\n-> 11110--- xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class E. This is not used for computers.\n\n-> Range of class A for MSB: 0 - 127\n-> Range of class B for MSB: 128 - 191\n-> Range of class C for MSB: 192 - 223\n-> Range of class D for MSB: 224 - 239\n-> Range of class E for MSB: 240 - 255\n\n-> If host bits are all 1's. Then it is called \"(Direct) Broadcast address\". As it sends data to all hosts in the network.\n-> Host bits are all 0's => \"Network ID\".\n-> 255.255.255.255 => \"Limited Broadcast Address\" => used by a host in it's own network to broadcast to all hosts in the same network.\n\n\nLecture 2: Classless addressing\n\n-> Classfull addressing because of it's rigid classification wastes a number of addresses while limiting number of networks(class A and B) and \n    limits the number of hosts as well (Class C)\n-> Classfull addressing is completely depreceated and is not used anymore.\n\n-> Classless Addressing: There is no rigid prefix bits for network id. It can be configured based on customer needs.\n-> 233.42.23.18/12 => the 12 after the slash represents the number of bits starting from left are given to the network id.\n\n\nLecture 3: Subentting\n\n-> The process of dividing the network into smaller subnetworks by borrowing some of the host bits and use that portion as subnet ID.\n-> If you want to have n subnetworks, then you need ceil(log(n)) bits from host bits to represent sub network. Each subnet will have equal number of hosts.\n-> But, you can also have variable length subnets.   \n\n\nLecture: Quiz 1 on Subnetting\n\n\nLecture 4: More In Subnetting\n\n \nLecture: Supernetting and Misconceptions\n\n\nLecture: Quiz 2 Subnetting and Supernetting\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Module 1: IP addressing, Subnetting and Supernetting\n\nLecture 1: Introduction to IP addressing\n\n-> IP Addresses are unique addresses given to computers that are connected to internet.\n-> They are given based on the link the computer is connected to.\n-> It has 32 bits. And the address itself is divided into two parts: Network ID and Host ID.\n\n\n-> Classful addressing: The IP addresses are divided into 5 classes based on the Most Significant Byte of the IP address.\n-> 0------- xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class A. Only MSB is part of Network ID.\n-> 10------ xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class B. The two MSB are part of Network ID.\n-> 110----- xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class C. The three MSB are part of Network ID.\n-> 1110---- xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class D. This is not used for computers.\n-> 11110--- xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx xxxxxxxx => class E. This is not used for computers.\n\n-> Range of class A for MSB: 0 - 127\n-> Range of class B for MSB: 128 - 191\n-> Range of class C for MSB: 192 - 223\n-> Range of class D for MSB: 224 - 239\n-> Range of class E for MSB: 240 - 255\n\n-> If host bits are all 1's. Then it is called \"(Direct) Broadcast address\". As it sends data to all hosts in the network.\n-> Host bits are all 0's => \"Network ID\".\n-> 255.255.255.255 => \"Limited Broadcast Address\" => used by a host in it's own network to broadcast to all hosts in the same network.\n\n\nLecture 2: Classless addressing\n\n-> Classfull addressing because of it's rigid classification wastes a number of addresses while limiting number of networks(class A and B) and \n    limits the number of hosts as well (Class C)\n-> Classfull addressing is completely depreceated and is not used anymore.\n\n-> Classless Addressing: There is no rigid prefix bits for network id. It can be configured based on customer needs.\n-> 233.42.23.18/12 => the 12 after the slash represents the number of bits starting from left are given to the network id.\n\n\nLecture 3: Subentting\n\n-> The process of dividing the network into smaller subnetworks by borrowing some of the host bits and use that portion as subnet ID.\n-> If you want to have n subnetworks, then you need ceil(log(n)) bits from host bits to represent sub network. Each subnet will have equal number of hosts.\n-> But, you can also have variable length subnets.   \n\n\nLecture: Quiz 1 on Subnetting\n\n\nLecture 4: More In Subnetting\n\n \nLecture: Supernetting and Misconceptions\n\n\nLecture: Quiz 2 Subnetting and Supernetting\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
