      "text": "Module 2: Data Link Layer\n\nLecture 5: Layering in Computer Networks\n\n-> Data Link Layer does these:\n    - Framing\n    - Physical Addressing\n    - Error Control\n    - Access Control\n\n-> Network Layer does these:\n    - Logical Addressing\n    - Routing\n    - Fragmentation and Re-assembly\n\n-> Transport Layer does these:\n    - Segmentation and Re-assembly\n    - Port addressing\n    - flow and error control\n\nLecture 6: Switching and Framing in Data Link Layer\n\n-> Circuit Switching\n-> Packet Switching\n\n-> Data Link Layer is implemented in the hardware.\n-> Framing: \n    - Fixed Length\n    - Variable Length\n        - Byte Stuffing\n        - Bit Stuffing\n\n\nLecture 7: Error Control Methods\n\n-> Types of Errors:\n    - Single Bit Error\n    - Burst Error\n\n-> Error Control involves: Error Detection and Error Correction\n\n-> Error Detection\n    - Single Bit Parity Codes\n    - CRC\n    - Checksum\n\n-> Error Correction\n    - Hamming Codes\n\nSingle Bit Parity Codes:\n-> Add an extra bit based on even parity or odd parity\n-> To detect you just check the parity of the code at receiver's end.\n-> Since, only parity is checked, it can false check the codes.\n\nHamming Distance:\n-> Hamming Distance of two codes is the number of bit positions that differ in the given codes.\n-> Given a set of codes, the minimum distance between any pair of valid codes is the \"Minimum Hamming Distance\"(dmin)\n-> A Channel can specify the most number of single bit errors that can occur per codeword. \n-> So, if a channel can produce atmost \"s\" errors, then \"dmin\" of valid codewords must be >= \"2*s + 1\"\n-> If \"dmin\" is the hamming distance, then a channel must produce atmost floor((dmin - 1) / 2). Which is also\n    \"The number of bits that we can correct\"\n-> If a code is \"c\" error correcting and \"d\" error detecting (d >= c) then dmin >= (c + d + 1)\n\n\n\nLecture 8a,b,c : Single Error Correcting Code and Hamming Codes\n\n-> Hamming Codes are Single Error Correcting (SEC) codes.\n-> To detect or correct errors, we must map valid \"data words\" to a larger space of \"codewords\" leaving \"empty space\" between them\n-> To correct one error we must require a minimum hamming distance of 3.\n\n-> To build ans SEC, we take an m-bit message and add \"r\" redundant \"parity bits\", creating a total codeword length\n    of n = m + r. This is known as (n, m) code.\n-> Every time an error occurs, it could happen in any of the n bits or there could be zero errors. So, we have n+1 possible scenarios.\n-> And we need the \"r\" parity bits to represent them uniquely. Since \"r\" bits can represent 2^r distinct states,\n    we get the \"Hmming Bound Inequality\", 2^r >= m + r + 1\n\n-> A 7-Bit code word will have the following form:\n    P1 P2 D3 P4 D4 D5 D6 D7 => Parity bits are always at the positions of powers of 2.\n-> We can divide the bits in above form into \"number of parity bits\" groups, each for one parity bit.\n-> The positions whose index in binary representation has the same bit set to 1 as the Parity bit binary representation form a group.\n    And they form the value of parity bit.\n-> For even Parity: \n    - P1 = D3 ^ D5 ^ D7\n    - P2 = D3 ^ D6 ^ D7\n    - P4 = D5 ^ D6 ^ D7\n-> When the receiver gets a codeword, it calculates a value called the \"Syndrome Vector\"(S).\n    S = [S4, S2, S1], where Si = Pi ^ Group of Pi\n-> The binary valud of S, represents the \"Bit Position\" where the error occured. \n-> If Odd parity is being used, complement of Syndrome, represents the Bit position where the error occurred.\n\n-> Because of grouping, chaning any bit automatically forms a valid codeword that has a hamming distance of 3 from the given valid codeword.\n    This makes sure to leave the \"empty space\" of invalid codewords.\n-> And when receiver receives a codeword which falls in such \"empty space\", the error is detected and corrected.\n\n\n\n\nLecture 9: CRC\n\n-> d(x) - dataword\n    c(x) - codeword\n    g(x) - generator\n    e(x) - error\n\n-> If generator has r + 1, then add r zeros to the end of dataword and divide with generator. The remainder will have\n    r bits. Append the remainder to dataword. The result is codeword.\n\n-> We don't use subtraction at each step of the division, instead we use something called \"Modulo 2 Addition/Subtraction\" (The sign doesn't matter).\n    Which is nothing but xor of bits at each position.\n-> This produces a series of codewords called Gaolis Field. They exhibit the Cyclic property which says shifting of \n    bits give another codeword.\n\n-> We can represent the generator, codeword and error interms of polynomial, with the powers being the bit positions.\n\n-> The receiver receives r(x) = c(x) + e(x)\n-> The reciver divides r(x) with g(x). If the remainder is non-zero then there is error.\n-> So, e(x) mod g(x) must be non-zero, to say the codeword is corrupted or to say error can be detected.\n\n-> g(x) = 1 + x. To check if some c(x) can be divided by 1 + x, substitute x = 1 in c(x), if the result is zero,\n    it cannot be divided and hence, the error cannot be detected.\n-> 1 + x divides all error-codewords with even number of errors. Hence, all even bit errors cannot be detected with 1 + x as generator.\n-> 1 + x cannot divide error-codewords with odd number of errors. Hence, all odd errors can be detected by 1 + x.\n\n-> The choosing of generator makes all the difference in the accuracy of error detection. This is very math heavy.\n    And there are generators called CRC-8, CRC-10, CRC-12, CRC-16, CRC-CCITT, CRC-32 invented by scientists which give\n    great accuracy.\n\n\n\nLecture 10: Checksum and Netowrk Measurements\n\n-> Split the Data into 16 bits groups. Add them all, Wrap the carry and add it to the result. Complement the result.\n-> It is called \"Check Sum\".\n-> The sender sends data and checksum to the receiver. \n-> The reciever adds data and checksum in the similar fashion. If the result is all 1's. There is probably no error.\n\n\n-> Bandwidth (Bytes/Sec): Number of bits that can be transmitted or can be put on the wire per unit time is called Bandwidth.\n-> Transmission Delay (Sec): Time taken to push all the bits of a packet onto the link.\n    - Packet Size/Bandwidth\n-> Propagation Delay (Sec): Time taken to move one bit from one end of the link to other.\n    - Link Length/Speed\n-> End-to-End Delay: Summation of all Delays.\n    Transmission + Propagation + Queing + Processing.\n\n\n\nLecture 12: Flow Control Method\n\n\n\n\nLecture 20: Media Access Control Protocols - Aloha\n\n-> A distributed algorithm running at nodes to determine who sould transmit data over a shared link\n-> Channel Paritioning : FDMA, TDMA, CDMA\n-> Turn Taking: Polling, Token Passing\n-> Random Access: Aloha, CSMA CD\n\nAloha:\n->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Module 2: Data Link Layer\n\nLecture 5: Layering in Computer Networks\n\n-> Data Link Layer does these:\n    - Framing\n    - Physical Addressing\n    - Error Control\n    - Access Control\n\n-> Network Layer does these:\n    - Logical Addressing\n    - Routing\n    - Fragmentation and Re-assembly\n\n-> Transport Layer does these:\n    - Segmentation and Re-assembly\n    - Port addressing\n    - flow and error control\n\nLecture 6: Switching and Framing in Data Link Layer\n\n-> Circuit Switching\n-> Packet Switching\n\n-> Data Link Layer is implemented in the hardware.\n-> Framing: \n    - Fixed Length\n    - Variable Length\n        - Byte Stuffing\n        - Bit Stuffing\n\n\nLecture 7: Error Control Methods\n\n-> Types of Errors:\n    - Single Bit Error\n    - Burst Error\n\n-> Error Control involves: Error Detection and Error Correction\n\n-> Error Detection\n    - Single Bit Parity Codes\n    - CRC\n    - Checksum\n\n-> Error Correction\n    - Hamming Codes\n\nSingle Bit Parity Codes:\n-> Add an extra bit based on even parity or odd parity\n-> To detect you just check the parity of the code at receiver's end.\n-> Since, only parity is checked, it can false check the codes.\n\nHamming Distance:\n-> Hamming Distance of two codes is the number of bit positions that differ in the given codes.\n-> Given a set of codes, the minimum distance between any pair of valid codes is the \"Minimum Hamming Distance\"(dmin)\n-> A Channel can specify the most number of single bit errors that can occur per codeword. \n-> So, if a channel can produce atmost \"s\" errors, then \"dmin\" of valid codewords must be >= \"2*s + 1\"\n-> If \"dmin\" is the hamming distance, then a channel must produce atmost floor((dmin - 1) / 2). Which is also\n    \"The number of bits that we can correct\"\n-> If a code is \"c\" error correcting and \"d\" error detecting (d >= c) then dmin >= (c + d + 1)\n\n\n\nLecture 8a,b,c : Single Error Correcting Code and Hamming Codes\n\n-> Hamming Codes are Single Error Correcting (SEC) codes.\n-> To detect or correct errors, we must map valid \"data words\" to a larger space of \"codewords\" leaving \"empty space\" between them\n-> To correct one error we must require a minimum hamming distance of 3.\n\n-> To build ans SEC, we take an m-bit message and add \"r\" redundant \"parity bits\", creating a total codeword length\n    of n = m + r. This is known as (n, m) code.\n-> Every time an error occurs, it could happen in any of the n bits or there could be zero errors. So, we have n+1 possible scenarios.\n-> And we need the \"r\" parity bits to represent them uniquely. Since \"r\" bits can represent 2^r distinct states,\n    we get the \"Hmming Bound Inequality\", 2^r >= m + r + 1\n\n-> A 7-Bit code word will have the following form:\n    P1 P2 D3 P4 D4 D5 D6 D7 => Parity bits are always at the positions of powers of 2.\n-> We can divide the bits in above form into \"number of parity bits\" groups, each for one parity bit.\n-> The positions whose index in binary representation has the same bit set to 1 as the Parity bit binary representation form a group.\n    And they form the value of parity bit.\n-> For even Parity: \n    - P1 = D3 ^ D5 ^ D7\n    - P2 = D3 ^ D6 ^ D7\n    - P4 = D5 ^ D6 ^ D7\n-> When the receiver gets a codeword, it calculates a value called the \"Syndrome Vector\"(S).\n    S = [S4, S2, S1], where Si = Pi ^ Group of Pi\n-> The binary valud of S, represents the \"Bit Position\" where the error occured. \n-> If Odd parity is being used, complement of Syndrome, represents the Bit position where the error occurred.\n\n-> Because of grouping, chaning any bit automatically forms a valid codeword that has a hamming distance of 3 from the given valid codeword.\n    This makes sure to leave the \"empty space\" of invalid codewords.\n-> And when receiver receives a codeword which falls in such \"empty space\", the error is detected and corrected.\n\n\n\n\nLecture 9: CRC\n\n-> d(x) - dataword\n    c(x) - codeword\n    g(x) - generator\n    e(x) - error\n\n-> If generator has r + 1, then add r zeros to the end of dataword and divide with generator. The remainder will have\n    r bits. Append the remainder to dataword. The result is codeword.\n\n-> We don't use subtraction at each step of the division, instead we use something called \"Modulo 2 Addition/Subtraction\" (The sign doesn't matter).\n    Which is nothing but xor of bits at each position.\n-> This produces a series of codewords called Gaolis Field. They exhibit the Cyclic property which says shifting of \n    bits give another codeword.\n\n-> We can represent the generator, codeword and error interms of polynomial, with the powers being the bit positions.\n\n-> The receiver receives r(x) = c(x) + e(x)\n-> The reciver divides r(x) with g(x). If the remainder is non-zero then there is error.\n-> So, e(x) mod g(x) must be non-zero, to say the codeword is corrupted or to say error can be detected.\n\n-> g(x) = 1 + x. To check if some c(x) can be divided by 1 + x, substitute x = 1 in c(x), if the result is zero,\n    it cannot be divided and hence, the error cannot be detected.\n-> 1 + x divides all error-codewords with even number of errors. Hence, all even bit errors cannot be detected with 1 + x as generator.\n-> 1 + x cannot divide error-codewords with odd number of errors. Hence, all odd errors can be detected by 1 + x.\n\n-> The choosing of generator makes all the difference in the accuracy of error detection. This is very math heavy.\n    And there are generators called CRC-8, CRC-10, CRC-12, CRC-16, CRC-CCITT, CRC-32 invented by scientists which give\n    great accuracy.\n\n\n\nLecture 10: Checksum and Netowrk Measurements\n\n-> Split the Data into 16 bits groups. Add them all, Wrap the carry and add it to the result. Complement the result.\n-> It is called \"Check Sum\".\n-> The sender sends data and checksum to the receiver. \n-> The reciever adds data and checksum in the similar fashion. If the result is all 1's. There is probably no error.\n\n\n-> Bandwidth (Bytes/Sec): Number of bits that can be transmitted or can be put on the wire per unit time is called Bandwidth.\n-> Transmission Delay (Sec): Time taken to push all the bits of a packet onto the link.\n    - Packet Size/Bandwidth\n-> Propagation Delay (Sec): Time taken to move one bit from one end of the link to other.\n    - Link Length/Speed\n-> End-to-End Delay: Summation of all Delays.\n    Transmission + Propagation + Queing + Processing.\n\n\n\nLecture 12: Flow Control Method\n\n\n\n\nLecture 20: Media Access Control Protocols - Aloha\n\n-> A distributed algorithm running at nodes to determine who sould transmit data over a shared link\n-> Channel Paritioning : FDMA, TDMA, CDMA\n-> Turn Taking: Polling, Token Passing\n-> Random Access: Aloha, CSMA CD\n\nAloha:\n->",
